Step 1: Web Application (Development & Production)
A. Running in Local Development Mode

In two terminal tabs:

powershell
# Tab 1: Start Backend (Port 5000)
cd "D:\(!SanjayASK Projects\BANK-101\BANK101\backend"
npm run dev
# Tab 2: Start Frontend (Port 3000 with auto-proxy to 5000)
cd "D:\(!SanjayASK Projects\BANK-101\BANK101\frontend"
npm run dev

Open http://localhost:3000
 in your browser.

B. Building for Production Web
powershell
cd "D:\(!SanjayASK Projects\BANK-101\BANK101\frontend"
npm run build
The production assets are output to BANK101/frontend/dist/.
You can host dist/ on Netlify, Vercel, AWS S3/CloudFront, or have the Express backend serve it as static files.
Step 2: Building the Android APK

The native Android project has been initialized and synced at BANK101/frontend/android.

Choose the method that fits your workflow:

Option A: Android Studio (Recommended for Visual Testing & USB Debugging)
Open Android Studio.
Select Open and choose folder: D:\(!SanjayASK Projects\BANK-101\BANK101\frontend\android
Android Studio will automatically resolve Gradle dependencies and set up the SDK.
From the top menu, click: Build > Build Bundle(s) / APK(s) > Build APK(s)
A popup will appear in the bottom-right: click locate to access app-debug.apk. You can transfer and install this APK on any Android phone.
Option B: GitHub Actions (Zero Local Setup — Cloud Build)

A CI/CD workflow is located at .github/workflows/build-apk.yml.

Initialize a git repository and push your project to GitHub (or your preferred remote):
powershell
git init
git add .
git commit -m "feat: complete BANK-101 platform with native Android support"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin <your-github-repo-url>
git push -u origin main
Navigate to your GitHub repository and open the Actions tab.
Select the Build Android APK & Web App workflow.
Once completed (approx. 2-3 minutes), download the ready-to-install BANK101-Android-APK directly from the Artifacts section.
Option C: Local Command Line (Requires Android SDK)

If you configure ANDROID_HOME pointing to your Android SDK:

powershell
# 1. Point to Java 17 (installed on your system)
$env:JAVA_HOME = "C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-17"
# 2. Point to your Android SDK location
$env:ANDROID_HOME = "C:\Users\<User>\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk"
# 3. Build Web & Sync to Android
cd "D:\(!SanjayASK Projects\BANK-101\BANK101\frontend"
npm run build
npm run cap:sync
# 4. Compile APK
cd "android"
.\gradlew.bat assembleDebug

The compiled APK will be created at: BANK101/frontend/android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk

Step 3: Connecting the Android App to the Backend

When running on an Android device or emulator, localhost refers to the mobile device itself, not your development PC.

For Local Testing over Wi-Fi:

Check your computer's local Wi-Fi IP address (e.g. ipconfig -> 192.168.1.15).
Create a .env.production file in BANK101/frontend:
ini
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.15:5000/api/v1
Re-run npm run build && npm run cap:sync before generating the APK.
Note: android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" is enabled in AndroidManifest.xml so Android allows local HTTP connections without requiring an SSL certificate during development.

For Cloud Production:

Point VITE_API_BASE_URL to your live domain:
ini
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.yourbank101domain.com/api/v1